plained, from the Skt., as ‘without a husband,’ as if Skt. ''vidhavā'' were < ''vi'', without, + ''dhava'', husband; but it is more prob. derived from the root (Skt.) ''vindh'', lack. The L. ''viduus'', lacking, deprived of, is prob. developed from the fem. ''vidua'', taken as adj., widowed, deprived. Similarly the words for ‘widower’ are derived from those for ‘widow.’ From L. ''viduus'' are ult. E. ''void'', ''avoid'', etc.] '''1.''' A woman who has lost her husband by death. {{smaller|In the early church, widows formed a separate class or order, whose duties were devotion and the care of the orphans, the sick, and prisoners.}}
{{smaller block|And whan the Queen and alle the othere noble Ladyes sawen that thei weren alle ''Wydewes'', and that alle the rialle Blood was lost, thei armed hem, and, as Creatures out of Wytt, thei slowen alle the men of the Contrey that weren laft.
{{right|''Mandeville'', Travels, p. 154.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>We’ll throw his castell down,
And make a ''widowe'' o’ his gaye ladye.</poem>
{{right|''Sang of the Outlaw Murray'' (Child’s Ballads, VI. 23).}}}}
{{smaller block|''Widow'' is also used attributively (now only colloquially): as, “a ''widow'' woman,” 2 Sam. xiv. 5.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>How may we content
This ''widow'' lady?</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', K. John, ii. 1. 548.}}}}
{{smaller block|Who has the paternal power whilst the ''widow'' queen is with child?
{{right|''Locke'', Of Government, § 123.}}}}
'''2.''' A European geometrid moth, ''Cidaria luctuata'', more fully called ''mourning widow'': an English collectors’ name.—'''3.''' In some ''card-games'', an additional hand dealt to the table, sometimes face up, sometimes not.—{{smaller|'''Hempen widow.''' See ''hempen''.—'''Locality of a widow.''' See ''locality''.—'''Mournful widow, mourning widow.''' See ''mournful-widow'', ''mourning-widow''.—'''Widow bewitched,''' a woman living apart from her husband; a grass-widow.}}

readings.c2s2:
¶
{{smaller block|What can you be able to do, that would be more grateful to them, than if they should see you divorced from your husband; a widow, nay, to live (a ''widow bewitcht'') worse than a widow; for widows may marry again.
{{right|''Bailey'', tr. of Colloquies of Erasmus, p. 136. (''Davies.'')}}}}
{{smaller block|Ay! and yo’ were Sylvia Robson, and as bonny and light-hearted a lass as any in all t’ Riding, though now yo’re a poor ''widow bewitched''. ''Mrs. Gaskell'', Sylvia’s Lovers, xxxix.}}
{{smaller|'''Widow’s chamber,''' the apparel and furniture of the bed-chamber of the widow of a London freeman, to which she was formerly entitled.—'''Widows’ lawn,''' a kind of fine thin muslin, made originally for widows’ caps. [Eng.]—'''Widow’s man.''' See the quotations.}}
{{smaller block|As to Square, who was in his person what is called a jolly fellow, or a ''widow’s man'', he easily reconciled his choice to the eternal fitness of things.
{{right|''Fielding'', Tom Jones, iii. 6. (''Davies.'')}}}}
{{smaller block|''Widow’s men'' are imaginary sailors, borne on the books, and receiving pay and prize-money, which is appropriated to Greenwich Hospital.
{{right|''Marryat'', Peter Simple, vii., note. (''Davies.'')}}}}
{{smaller|'''Widow’s mantle.''' See ''mantle''.—'''Widow’s ring.''' See ''ring''<sup>1</sup>.—'''Widows’ silk,''' a silk fabric made with a very dull surface, and considered especially fit for mourning.—'''Widow’s weeds,''' the mourning-dress of a widow.}}

'''widow'''<sup>1</sup> (wid′ō), ''v. t.'' [< ''widow''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.''] '''1.''' To reduce to the condition of a widow; bereave of a husband or mate: commonly in the past participle.
{{smaller block|<poem>In this city he
Hath ''widow’d'' and unchilded many a one.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Cor., v. 6. 153.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>We orphaned many children,
And ''widowed'' many women.</poem>
{{right|''Peacock'', War-Song of Dinas Vawr.}}}}
'''2.''' To endow with a widow’s right. [Rare.]

readings.c2s3:
{{smaller block|For his possessions,<br>Although by confiscation they are ours,<br>We do instate and ''widow'' you withal,<br>To buy you a better husband.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. for M., v. 1. 429.}}}}
'''3.''' Figuratively, to deprive of anything regarded as analogous to a husband; bereave: sometimes with ''of.''
{{smaller block|The ''widow’d'' isle in mourning<br>Dries up her tears.
{{right|''Dryden.''}}
Trees ''of'' their shrivell’d fruits<br>Are ''widow’d.''
{{right|''J. Philips'', Cider, ii. 74.}}}}
'''4†.''' To survive as the widow of; be widow to.
{{smaller block|Let me be married to three kings in a forenoon, and ''widow'' them all.
{{right|''Shak.'', A. and C., i. 2. 27.}}}}

'''widow<sup>2</sup>''' (wid′ō), ''n.'' [Short for ''widow-bird.''] A whidah-bird.—{{smaller|'''Mourning widow''', a whidah-bird of the genus ''Coliuspasser.'' See ''Viduinæ.''—'''Widow of paradise''', one of the whidah-birds. See ''Vidua'' (with cut).}}

'''widow-bench''' (wid′ō-bench), ''n.'' That share which a widow is allowed of her husband’s estate, besides her jointure. ''Wharton.''

'''widow-bird''' (wid′ō-bėrd), ''n.'' [An accom. form (simulating E. ''widow''<sup>1</sup>) of ''whidah-bird.''] Same as ''whidah-bird.'' Also ''widow-finch.''

'''widow-burning''' (wid′ō-bėr″ning), ''n.'' Same as ''suttee'', 2.

'''widow-duck''' (wid′ō-duk), ''n.'' The Vicissy duck, ''Dendrocygna viduata'', one of the best-known tree-ducks.

