'''widowerhood''' (wid′ọ̄-ėr-hu̇d), ''n.'' [< ''widower''<sup>1</sup> + ''-hood''.] The condition of a widower.
{{smaller block|Ine spoushod, other ine ''wodewehod''.
{{right|''Ayenbite of Inwyt'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 185.}}}}

'''widow-finch''' (wid′ō-finch), ''n.'' Same as ''whidah-finch''.

'''widowhead†''' (wid′ọ̄-hed), ''n.'' [< ''widow''<sup>1</sup> + ''-head''.] Widowhood.
{{smaller block|Virginity, wedlock, and ''widowhead'' are none better than other, to be saved by, in their own nature.
{{right|''Tyndale'', Ans. to Sir T. More, etc. (Parker Soc., 1850), p. 157.}}}}
{{smaller block|Upon you, who are a member of the spouse of Christ, the church, there can fall no ''widowhead'', nor orphanage upon those children to whom God is father.
{{right|''Donne'', Letters, lxxvi.}}}}

